    = help: change the return type to `!U`, or handle it here with `with` or `catch` (spec §9.5)

error[DZ0501]: `with` needs a `?T` or `!T`, found `i32`
    ┌─ D:/OdinProjects/Dazzle/tests/workspace/typeerrors/src/errors.dz:152:11
    │
152 │     with (n) {
    │           ^ this cannot be absent or fail
    │
    = help: `with` binds the success payload of an optional or fallible value (spec §9.4)

error[DZ0501]: `?T` has no failure value to bind as `e`
    ┌─ D:/OdinProjects/Dazzle/tests/workspace/typeerrors/src/errors.dz:160:14
    │
160 │     } else { e ->
    │              ^ nothing to bind here
    │
    = help: only `!T` carries an `Error`; write `else { ... }` with no binding (spec §9.4)

error[DZ0521]: `none` needs an optional type here
    ┌─ D:/OdinProjects/Dazzle/tests/workspace/typeerrors/src/errors.dz:166:13
    │
166 │     let x = none
    │             ^^^^ nothing here expects a `?T`
    │
    = help: `none` is the empty case of `?T` (spec §9.1)

error[DZ0501]: `i32` cannot be iterated
    ┌─ D:/OdinProjects/Dazzle/tests/workspace/typeerrors/src/errors.dz:171:14
    │
171 │     for i in n {
    │              ^ not iterable
    │
    = help: `for` works over ranges, arrays and slices; user types need the `Iterate` trait, which arrives with generics (spec §8.5)
